  "requirements": [
    {
      "name": "Implicit Differentiation",
      "necessity": "To differentiate an equation implicitly with respect to x, we need to apply the chain rule and product rule where necessary."
   ,
    "search_query": "implicit differentiation rules"
    },
    {
      "name": "Chain Rule",
      "necessity": "The chain rule is essential for differentiating composite functions. It states that if y = f(g(x)), then dy/dx = f'(g(x)) * g'(x). This will be used to differentiate sin(xy) with respect to x."
    ,
    "search_query": "chain rule"
    },
    {
      "name": "Product Rule",
      "necessity": "The product rule is needed when differentiating a product of two functions. It states that if y = u(x)v(x), then dy/dx = u'(x)v(x) + u(x)v'(x). This will be used to differentiate xy with respect to x."
    ,
    "search_query": "product rule"
    },
    {
      "name": "Differentiation of Trigonometric Functions",
      "necessity": "To differentiate sin(xy), we need to know the derivative of sin(u) where u is a function of x. This requires understanding that dsin(u)/dx = cos(u) * du/dx."
    ,
    "search_query": "derivative of sine"
    },
    {
      "name": "Differentiation of Exponential Functions",
      "necessity": "To differentiate y^2, we need to know the power rule for differentiation. This states that if y = x^n, then dy/dx = nx^(n-1). This will be used to differentiate y^2 with respect to x."
    ,
    "search_query": "power rule"
    }
  ]
}
